<%= header_section(@product_files.size == 1 ? "A video in your product can't be played" : "Videos in your product can't be played") %>
<div>
  <p>
    <% if @product_files.size == 1 %>
      One of the videos in <%= @product.name %> can't be played by your customers. We couldn't read any video from the file after it finished uploading, so we were never able to prepare a playable version of it.
    <% else %>
      <%= @product_files.size %> of the videos in <%= @product.name %> can't be played by your customers. We couldn't read any video from these files after they finished uploading, so we were never able to prepare playable versions of them.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <% @product_files.each do |product_file| %>
      <li><%= product_file.display_name.presence || product_file.s3_filename %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This usually means the upload was incomplete or the file was damaged before it reached us, which we can't repair on our end. To fix it, re-upload
    <%= @product_files.size == 1 ? "the file" : "these files" %> to your product. Playing
    <%= @product_files.size == 1 ? "it" : "them" %> locally first is worth doing, since a file that won't play on your computer won't play for your customers either.
  </p>
  <p><%= link_to("Edit your product", edit_link_url(@product), class: "button primary") %></p>
  <p>Or you can go here: <%= link_to edit_link_url(@product), edit_link_url(@product) %></p>
  <p>
    Everything else in this product is unaffected, and your customers can still access the rest of the content.
  </p>
</div>
